{"summary":"Workspace conditions first, because they bound the review: figures/ contains only six Markdown briefs — there is no rendered-*.png for any figure — and review/figure-audit-evidence.json does not exist, so no pixel, OCR, leader, hatch-angle or cutting-plane-endpoint evidence could be checked. Every figure finding below is against the briefs read as the drawing specification. (The absent sheets and the 30 numerals absent from any drawing are already the two mechanical failures and are not re-reported as findings.) prior_art/INDEX.md lists no references and the draft contains no [REF:...] citation, so there is no citation issue; per the drafting brief, the art supplied to this draft may be incomplete.

On substance the draft tracks the inventor's disclosure closely. Every independent and dependent claim limitation traces to a disclosed sentence (shell/tray/module/lid, tray feet and lower return passage, inward-spaced tray walls and two side supply passages, rigid spacer frame with central inlet, two downward guide ducts and peripheral outlet openings, the closed passive path, lid rib into mating channel with registration, gasket, resilient feet on ledges, wick/pull tab/condensate channel, and the irreversible mechanical exposure indicator). I found no claim broader than the description, no element recited that the description omits, and consistent one-name-per-part usage across summary, description, claims, numerals.md and the briefs.

The defects are three geometric inconsistencies in the drawing briefs against the description and against each other — the central inlet opening is briefed opening toward the lid rather than toward the product region the text says air rises from; FIG. 5 places the condensate discharge opening at the shell floor in a region FIG. 2 does not contain it; FIG. 6 draws a phase-change cassette lying against the shell side wall in direct contact with the indicator stem, contradicting FIG. 3/FIG. 4 and adding a coupling relationship the disclosure states only as "thermally coupled" — plus four smaller wording and brief items. Each has a fix available in the existing text or briefs from disclosed matter.","findings":[{"severity":"major","category":"internal_logic","title":"Central inlet opening 46 is briefed and described as facing the lid, but the recited circulation has air rising from the product region below enter it","where":"figures/FIG-3.md ("Central inlet opening 46" bullet); draft/08-detailed-description.md, "The removable thermal module" (¶ beginning "The rigid spacer frame 42 includes") and "The registered passive circulation path"; figures/FIG-2.md","detail":"The disclosure says only "It includes a central inlet opening" and "Warm air rising from the product region enters the central inlet, divides along faces of the cassettes, descends through the guide ducts" — it does not place the opening. The candidate placed it "through its upper region" and FIG-3 briefs it as a gap in the upper web that is "open upward toward the lid 60", with the same brief stating that "The rib 62 is engaged in the channel 52 while the lid 60 lies across the upper web of the frame 42". In that geometry the only side of opening 46 facing away from the frame interior is closed off by the lid, so air rising from the product region cannot enter it, and the entrance from the tray is instead the frame's open underside. FIG-2 reads the same opening the opposite way, as facing the payload: "The interior of the tray 20 is open upward toward the central opening of the module 40." One of the two briefs must be wrong, and the description's placement of the inlet in the frame's "upper region" is itself an addition to the source. FIG-3 also routes the inter-cassette space to the ducts "around their lower parts", which lets air that entered from below leave at the bottom without descending along the cassette faces as the text recites.","evidence":"figures/FIG-3.md: "Central inlet opening 46: one rectangular gap through the upper web of the frame 42, at the centre of the width, open upward toward the lid 60 and downward into the frame interior." / "The space between the two cassettes 44 is continuous with the opening 46 above it and with both ducts 48 around their lower parts." — draft/08-detailed-description.md: "The rigid spacer frame 42 includes a central inlet opening 46 through its upper region" and "warm air rising from a product region within the removable product tray 20 enters the central inlet opening 46, divides along faces of the phase-change cassettes 44, descends through the downward guide ducts 48" — figures/FIG-2.md: "The interior of the tray 20 is open upward toward the central opening of the module 40."","fix":"In draft/08-detailed-description.md write "a central inlet opening 46 at the centre of the rigid spacer frame 42 and facing the product region within the removable product tray 20". In figures/FIG-3.md change the bullet to: "Central inlet opening 46: one rectangular gap at the centre of the width of the frame 42, opening downward toward the interior of the tray 20 and upward into the blank open space between the two cassettes 44, the lower web portions of the frame 42 closing the underside of the frame 42 on each side of it." and change the relationship line to: "The space between the two cassettes 44 is continuous with the opening 46 below it and with both ducts 48 across the upper parts of the cassettes 44, so that air entering the opening 46 passes along the opposed faces of the cassettes 44 and turns into the ducts 48.""},{"severity":"major","category":"figures_and_numerals","title":"FIG. 5 places condensate discharge opening 56 at the shell floor, in a region FIG. 2 does not contain it and where the guide ducts are not","where":"figures/FIG-5.md ("Condensate discharge opening 56" bullet and relationship list); figures/FIG-2.md (broken-circle paragraph); figures/FIG-4.md; draft/07-drawings.md, FIG. 5 sentence","detail":"FIG. 5 is expressly the enlargement of FIG. 2's right-hand lower circle, which FIG-2 briefs as enclosing only the floor trough and the lower right of the tray. FIG-5 nevertheless puts a hatched web spanning the upper part of that view, with a descending passage through it ending in opening 56 just above the channel — structure the parent view does not have at that location. It also contradicts the description and FIG. 4: opening 56 is defined as being at the lower end of downward guide duct 48, and duct 48 belongs to the module above the tray, where FIG-4 shows it "running downward and open at its lower end". The web is left "unnumbered" precisely because no numbered part can sit there. Naming it 42/48 would move the module to the shell floor; the correct correction is to show 56 where the duct lower end actually is.","evidence":"figures/FIG-5.md: "Condensate discharge opening 56: a downwardly opening gap in an unnumbered hatched web that spans the upper part of the view, at the lower end of a descending passage through that web, positioned above the channel 70." — figures/FIG-2.md: "one around the right trough of the shell floor 12 and the lower right of the tray 20" — draft/08-detailed-description.md: "Condensate discharge openings 56 at lower ends of the downward guide ducts 48 discharge condensate into the shallow condensate channel 70."","fix":"Delete the "Condensate discharge opening 56" bullet and the line "The condensate discharge opening 56 opens downward toward the trough of the channel 70." from figures/FIG-5.md and remove 56 from its numeral list. Add to figures/FIG-4.md: "Condensate discharge opening 56: a downwardly opening gap at the lower end of the duct 48 in the underside of the frame 42, opening downward above the upper end of the side supply passage 30." and add 56 to that sheet's numeral list. In draft/07-drawings.md end the FIG. 4 sentence "... bearing on a ledge of the insulated outer shell, and a condensate discharge opening at a lower end of a downward guide duct", and end the FIG. 5 sentence "... showing the shallow condensate channel and the removable absorbent wick." In draft/08-detailed-description.md open that paragraph "Referring to FIG. 4 and FIG. 5, ..."."},{"severity":"major","category":"figures_and_numerals","title":"FIG. 6 draws a phase-change cassette lying against the shell side wall and touching the indicator stem, contradicting FIG. 3 and FIG. 4 and going beyond "thermally coupled"","where":"figures/FIG-6.md ("Phase-change cassette 44" bullet and relationship list); against draft/08-detailed-description.md, "The removable thermal module" and "Mechanical exposure indicator"; figures/FIG-3.md, figures/FIG-4.md","detail":"Both sources say only that the stem is "thermally coupled to one phase-change cassette"; neither states contact, nor that a cassette lies against the shell wall. Everywhere else in the application the cassettes are held inside the rigid spacer frame with the guide duct and the frame's outboard web between a cassette and the shell side wall, so nothing at the inner surface of the shell side wall can be a cassette. The FIG. 6 geometry therefore both adds an undisclosed structural relationship and contradicts the described module construction. The description itself stays neutral, so the correction belongs in the brief. (A prior review suggested this contact wording; a reviewer suggestion is not inventor disclosure.)","evidence":"figures/FIG-6.md: "Phase-change cassette 44: a slab-like body inside the carrier at the right of the view ... lying against the inner surface of the shell side wall 14 with the inner end of the stem 82 in contact with its facing surface." and "The stem 82 extends from the facing surface of the cassette 44, through the thickness of the shell side wall 14, into the interior of the indicator 80, so that the stem 82 and the cassette 44 are in contact." — figures/FIG-4.md: "Rigid spacer frame 42: ... the outboard web lying inboard of the shell side wall 14" and "Downward guide duct 48: a blank open upright passage inboard of the outboard web of the frame 42" — figures/FIG-3.md: "Downward guide duct 48: two blank open upright passages ... each between the outboard surface of one cassette 44 and the nearer outboard web of the frame 42" — input/disclosure.md: "An indicator stem is thermally coupled to one phase-change cassette and retained by a temperature-responsive latch."","fix":"In figures/FIG-6.md change the cassette bullet to: "Phase-change cassette 44: a slab-like body inside the carrier at the right of the view, drawn with its own outline and its own hatching pattern, spaced inboard of the inner surface of the shell side wall 14, the inner end of the stem 82 lying at that inner surface and thermally coupled to the facing surface of the cassette 44." and change the relationship line to: "The stem 82 extends from the interior of the indicator 80 through the thickness of the shell side wall 14 to its inner surface, where it is thermally coupled to the cassette 44.""},{"severity":"minor","category":"disclosure_fidelity","title":""Clear of the upper edge" states a spacing during registration that the source states only as sealing after registration","where":"draft/08-detailed-description.md, "Lid-driven registration and retention" (¶ beginning "A compressible lid gasket 64"); draft/09-claims.md, claim 2","detail":"The disclosure and the conversation say only that the gasket "seals against the upper edge of the shell after registration". The candidate converts that timing statement into a positive geometric condition — the gasket being clear of (spaced from) the upper edge while the rib is registering — which is narrower and more specific than the source; a gasket in initial uncompressed contact would satisfy the disclosure but not this wording. The fix keeps the disclosed ordering without asserting the spacing.","evidence":"draft/08-detailed-description.md: "The depending indexing rib 62 engages the mating channel 52 and completes the registration while the compressible lid gasket 64 is still clear of the upper edge 16" — draft/09-claims.md claim 2: "the depending indexing rib engaging the mating channel while the compressible lid gasket is clear of the upper edge" — input/disclosure.md: "A compressible lid gasket seals against the upper edge of the shell after registration."","fix":"In the description write: "The depending indexing rib 62 engages the mating channel 52 and completes the registration before the compressible lid gasket 64 seals against the upper edge 16 ...". In claim 2 write: "... the depending indexing rib engaging the mating channel and registering the removable thermal module before the compressible lid gasket seals against the upper edge, continued closing of the insulated lid thereafter compressing the compressible lid gasket against the upper edge.""},{"severity":"minor","category":"disclosure_fidelity","title":"Method paragraph fixes an order between pressing the resilient feet and compressing the gasket that no source gives","where":"draft/08-detailed-description.md, "Method of passively conditioning a payload" (first paragraph)","detail":"The disclosure states that the closing movement registers the module, that the gasket seals "after registration", and that "The same closing movement presses resilient feet of the spacer frame against ledges of the shell" — it says nothing about the feet being pressed before the gasket is compressed. The candidate's "and then" sequence adds a temporal relationship not in either source. Claims 13 and 14 do not recite it, so removing it costs nothing.","evidence":"draft/08-detailed-description.md: "shifts the removable thermal module 40 into the registered position in which the peripheral outlet openings 50 overlap the side supply passages 30, presses the resilient feet 54 against the ledges 18, and then compresses the compressible lid gasket 64 against the upper edge 16."","fix":"Write: "... shifts the removable thermal module 40 into the registered position in which the peripheral outlet openings 50 overlap the side supply passages 30, presses the resilient feet 54 against the ledges 18, and compresses the compressible lid gasket 64 against the upper edge 16 after that registration.""},{"severity":"minor","category":"internal_logic","title":"Description says the latch holds the spring; the disclosure and claim 9 say the latch retains the stem","where":"draft/08-detailed-description.md, "Mechanical exposure indicator" (first paragraph)","detail":"The sentence's subject is the spring 86, so as written the temperature-responsive latch 84 holds the spring. Everywhere else — the disclosure, the summary and claim 9 — the latch retains the indicator stem, and the spring is merely held in compression as a consequence. Small, but it is the one place the retention interface is stated differently.","evidence":"draft/08-detailed-description.md: "A spring 86 acts on the indicator stem 82 in the outward direction and is held by the temperature-responsive latch 84 while the phase-change cassette 44 remains in condition." — draft/09-claims.md claim 9: "a temperature-responsive latch retaining the indicator stem" — input/disclosure.md: "An indicator stem is thermally coupled to one phase-change cassette and retained by a temperature-responsive latch."","fix":"Write: "A spring 86 acts on the indicator stem 82 in the outward direction, and the temperature-responsive latch 84 retains the indicator stem 82 against that action while the phase-change cassette 44 remains in condition.""},{"severity":"minor","category":"figures_and_numerals","title":"Brief Description says FIG. 2 shows the circulation path, but FIG-2's brief specifies no flow indication","where":"draft/07-drawings.md, FIG. 2 sentence; figures/FIG-2.md","detail":"The Brief Description identifies FIG. 2 as showing the circulation path. The brief specifies only that the passages together form an open circuit; it briefs no arrows or other indication of the direction of flow, so the path as such is not shown. The path is fully disclosed, so flow arrows can simply be added to the brief.","evidence":"draft/07-drawings.md: "FIG. 2 is a schematic vertical sectional view of the carrier in a closed condition, taken on line 2-2 of FIG. 1, showing the lower return passage, the side supply passages, and the circulation path around the removable product tray." — figures/FIG-2.md: "The lower return passage 28, both side supply passages 30, the perforations 26, and the descending passages of the module 40 form one open circuit around the tray 20."","fix":"Add a bullet to figures/FIG-2.md: "Flow arrows: plain unnumbered arrows drawn in the blank open spaces, showing air passing upward from the interior of the tray 20 into the central opening of the module 40, downward through the two descending passages of the module 40, downward in each side supply passage 30, through the perforations 26, and along the lower return passage 28 back to the interior of the tray 20.""},{"severity":"minor","category":"figures_and_numerals","title":"Numbered parts are called by unnumbered generic names in two briefs","where":"figures/FIG-2.md ("Removable product tray 20" and "Removable thermal module 40" bullets); figures/FIG-4.md ("Side supply passage 30" bullet)","detail":"The tray feet 22, the downward guide ducts 48 and the tray side wall 24 are numbered parts in numerals.md and in the detailed description, but these briefs describe them as "short hatched supports", "two passages descending through it" and "the hatched upright panel inboard of it", with no name and no numeral. The same part should carry the same name in every view, and each of these numerals is otherwise carried only on one sheet.","evidence":"figures/FIG-2.md: "drawn as a hatched bottom panel and two shorter upright side panels, with short hatched supports depending from the bottom panel to the shell floor 12" and "with one opening through its upper face at the centre and two passages descending through it" — figures/FIG-4.md: "between the inner face of the shell side wall 14 and the hatched upright panel inboard of it"","fix":"In figures/FIG-2.md write "with tray feet 22 depending from the bottom panel to the shell floor 12" and "and two downward guide ducts 48 descending through it, each opening downward above one side supply passage 30", adding 22 and 48 to that sheet's numeral list. In figures/FIG-4.md write "between the inner face of the shell side wall 14 and the tray side wall 24 inboard of it", adding 24 to that sheet's numeral list."}]}
